<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <OutputType>Exe</OutputType>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <RootNamespace>CupriLex.Compiler</RootNamespace>
    <AssemblyName>CupriLex.Compiler</AssemblyName>
    <InvariantGlobalization>true</InvariantGlobalization>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!--
      A real JavaScript parser, and NO JavaScript engine. The distinction is the whole design:
      every tween is read out of the syntax tree and resolved by arithmetic, because a block that
      cannot be resolved must be refused rather than approximated, and an engine would happily
      approximate. Acornima is the maintained successor to Esprima.NET and is what Jint parses
      with, so it tracks the language rather than a snapshot of it.

      AngleSharp finds the inline scripts, pinned to the version CupriFace already resolves, so a
      document is read by one HTML parser on both sides of the comparison.
    -->
    <PackageReference Include="Acornima" Version="1.8.0" />
    <PackageReference Include="AngleSharp" Version="1.7.0" />

    <!--
      The CSS side of the same parser, for ONE question: what does the stylesheet already say this
      element's opacity and transform are? A tween to opacity 1 on an element the stylesheet
      authors at 0 is a fade in a browser and a still here, and 112 animations across 45 blocks
      were emitted flat for exactly that reason.

      DECLARED values, never computed ones. ComputeStyle() is the obvious call and it throws a
      NullReferenceException on `translate(-50%, -50%)`, the commonest centring idiom in this
      corpus - and a computed pixel value is the wrong answer anyway, because what a tween starts
      from is what the author wrote.

      Prerelease because that is the only form this package has ever had. Pinned exactly.
    -->
    <PackageReference Include="AngleSharp.Css" Version="1.0.0-beta.154" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
